      "content": "Yes! Because wavelength and the angle of diffraction are directly proportional, a shorter wavelength results in a smaller degree of diffraction. \n\n**Final Answer:** B\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{n\\lambda}{d}$ (derived for single-slit diffraction minima)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\theta$ is the angle of diffraction (the spread of the light pattern)\n  * $\\lambda$ is the wavelength of the light wave (in meters)\n  * $d$ is the width of the single slit (in meters)\n  * $n$ is the order of the minimum ($1, 2, 3...$)\n* **Why we use it:** Diffraction depends on how the wavelength compares to the size of the opening. Since blue light has a shorter wavelength ($\\lambda$) than red light, it bends less around the obstacle/slit of width $d$, resulting in a narrower diffraction pattern (smaller degree of diffraction).\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Remember the colour spectrum order (ROYGBIV). Red has the longest wavelength and bends the *most*; violet/blue has the shortest wavelength and bends the *least*!"
